  "preamble": "Rhotacism of the alveolar nasal: /n/ loses its nasal airflow and becomes the alveolar tap [ɾ]. There are two main contexts. Between vowels: Tosk Albanian, where Proto-Albanian intervocalic *n became r (*grunā > grurë 'wheat') while Gheg keeps n, and Istro-Romanian in words of Latin origin (bonus > bur). After a stop or nasal in an initial cluster: northern Irish and Scottish Gaelic kn, gn, mn > kr, gr, mr, often with a nasalized vowel after it.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Rhotacism, Denasalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Tosk Albanian",
      "languageFamily": "Albanian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *grunā",
          "to": "Tosk grurë 'wheat'",
          "note": "Albanian single r is the tap [ɾ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *wana",
          "to": "Tosk uri 'hunger'",
          "note": "Gheg uni keeps n."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *tsanidā",
          "to": "Tosk thëri 'nit'",
          "note": "Gheg thëni keeps n."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *alanā",
          "to": "Tosk llërë 'upper arm'",
          "note": "Gheg llânë keeps n."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *twana",
          "to": "Tosk tërë 'whole'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *wainā- 'wine'",
          "to": "Tosk verë [vɛɾə]",
          "note": "Gheg keeps venë."
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin arēna 'sand'",
          "to": "Tosk rërë",
          "note": "Gheg ranë; the rhotacism postdates Latin borrowing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Istro-Romanian",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin bonus 'good' (cf. Daco-Romanian bun)",
          "to": "bur /bur/",
          "note": "Intervocalic n became r in words of Latin origin"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin bene 'well' (cf. Daco-Romanian bine)",
          "to": "bire /bire/",
          "note": "Same change"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin canis, canem 'dog' (cf. Daco-Romanian câine)",
          "to": "câre /kəre/",
          "note": "Same change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Irish",
      "languageFamily": "Goidelic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Irish cnocc 'hill'",
          "to": "Ulster /kɾˠɞk/",
          "note": "/n/ after a stop becomes a tap in northern dialects; Munster keeps /kn̪ˠuk/."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Irish mná 'women'",
          "to": "Ulster /mˠɾˠãː/",
          "note": "After /m/, with the following vowel nasalized; Munster keeps /mˠn̪ˠɑː/."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Irish gnó 'business'",
          "to": "Connacht /ɡɾˠoː/",
          "note": "After /ɡ/; Munster keeps /ɡn̪ˠoː/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Scottish Gaelic",
      "languageFamily": "Goidelic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Irish cnocc 'hill'",
          "to": "cnoc /kʰɾɔ̃xk/",
          "note": "The cluster /kn/ became /kɾ/, with nasalization moved onto the vowel"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Irish cnáim 'bone'",
          "to": "cnàimh /kʰɾɛ̃ːv/ (Southern Hebrides, Skye)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
